                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Whenever I take a gamble and buy a pack of random Steam keys on eBay, I know what I'm getting: games that range from being so poor they can no longer be purchased or barely pass the bar where competent design goes.\n\nOn a side note: the only positive I can give for such bundles is that they boost up the total number of games in my library and that definitely helps getting total games count badges.\n\nA key for this game was among one such pack I bought. It immediately caught my eye. It was one of the most unique looking games I saw amongst the bunch. \n\nSo I booted it up and was surprised that it's a indie game that manages to actually be a surprisingly great experience that's worth playing through from start to finish.\n\nM1: A Death in the Desert is a visual novel set in a post-apocalyptic setting. It starts off with your protagonist, M, waking up in an abandoned hotel with no memory of how he got there. Your only clues as to why you are in the state you are in is a magnum with three bullets and a list of three names you are instructed to kill.\n\nFrom there you engage in a trek filled with intrigue, a bit of existentialist philosophy, some quirky humor and dialogue with the very eccentric residents of this game's post-apocalyptic world. Being that this is a standard visual novel, there's many choices you can pick from that result in branching paths that can lead you to one of five different endings too.\n\nAlong with having an engaging little tale that's well written, this game boasts an admittedly good looking pixel-based visual style along with a nicely composed soundtrack by indie composer Falcon Ave. It's one of the most surprising relaxing soundtrack I've heard in a post-apocalyptic definitely worth the extra small fee of 99 cents being asked to download it via a separate DLC.\n\nThere are, as with any video game, shortcomings. The chief one being that the game is very, and I do mean very, short. It's possible to get all five of this game's endings within the span of a single day. It also at times suffers from a lack of polish specifically in regards to its text which at times has noticeable grammar errors that could benefit from correction.\n\nStill, this is a definite example of a small yet still very nice gem in rough that's definitely worth its affordable asking price of $2.99 if you are in the mood for a quirky and enjoyably unorthodox post-apocalyptic story.\n\nMore impressive though is that this is the first visual novel that the developer Dracula's Cave has ever made. Having played quite a number of visual novels like this one, it's a good though still slightly off the mark initial effort that's executed just well enough it deserves polite little golf clap applause\n\nI hope to see more visual novels in the future from this game's developer. For a first time attempt, this is definitely a good effort and one you definite ought to check out if you're up for a short visual novel.",
